      "writtenOn": "2019-02-12T19:38:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In traditional syntax it\u0027d look like\n            ArrayList\u003cNetworkRegistrationState\u003e sanitizedStates \u003d\n                    new ArrayList\u003c\u003e(state.mNetworkRegistrationStates.size());\n            for (NetworkRegistrationState regState : state.mNetworkRegistrationStates) {\n                sanitizedStates.add(regState.sanitizeLocationInfo());\n            }\n            state.mNetworkRegistrationStates \u003d sanitizedStates;\n\nNot sure if that looks any cleaner. Also I don\u0027t think there\u0027s much of a performance impact since NetworkRegistrationState is a pretty heavyweight object whose allocations are gonna happen anyway. Do you think this is going to be a performance-critical piece of code? I can profile it and see if so",
